WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.959
Hello and welcome. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:01.959 --> 00:00:06.110
And in&nbsp;this episode, we're talking about chained comparisons in Python.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:09.171
By a chained comparison,&nbsp;I mean an expression like this

00:00:09.171 --> 00:00:11.084
where I have multiple comparisons.

00:00:11.084 --> 00:00:15.661
In this case, I have two less&nbsp;thans that appear in the same expression.

00:00:15.661 --> 00:00:17.546
So, this kind of thing is totally fine in Python.

00:00:17.546 --> 00:00:20.160
You can&nbsp;have as many of these strung together as you want

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.911
and you can mix and match operators.

00:00:21.911 --> 00:00:26.304
I can use&nbsp;less than, less than or equal to, equals, those kind of things.

00:00:26.304 --> 00:00:31.781
And as long as each of the things&nbsp;pairwise makes sense, like 5 less than equal to 10,

00:00:31.781 --> 00:00:36.720
as long as each of these things makes sense, then&nbsp;this is a valid thing you can do in Python.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.188
However, what it means is a little bit non-intuitive.

00:00:40.188 --> 00:00:44.473
Python&nbsp;tried to make things work a little bit more like the way that math works.

00:00:44.473 --> 00:00:50.655
So, this expression, x &lt; y &lt; z, is supposed to mean what it would in mathematical notation.

00:00:50.655 --> 00:00:53.840
Namely that, y&nbsp;is between x and z.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.193
This might be a little bit counter-intuitive especially if you're coming&nbsp;
from a different language,

00:00:58.193 --> 00:01:00.583
or if you're trying to comprehend, like:

00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:05.221
"Does this happen left to right&nbsp;and where do the parentheses go?" that kind of thing.

00:01:05.221 --> 00:01:11.454
So, if you tried to think about it and you said:&nbsp;Okay, I know y less than z returns a boolean.

00:01:11.454 --> 00:01:13.812
So, is this supposed to mean, like

00:01:13.812 --> 00:01:17.690
y &lt; z, okay&nbsp;1 is less than 2, that's true,

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:21.692
x is 0. 0 is less than true, that's true?

00:01:21.692 --> 00:01:26.343
And you know putting&nbsp;the parentheses over here wouldn't really make it any better.

00:01:26.343 --> 00:01:31.532
If I did this, you'd still be like&nbsp;comparing a true and a false versus another thing.

00:01:31.532 --> 00:01:35.614
But in Python, they sort of did away with this idea&nbsp;that

00:01:35.614 --> 00:01:39.886
you pick a left to right or right to left way of evaluating the parentheses

00:01:39.886 --> 00:01:43.347
and instead they&nbsp;made it mean following.

00:01:43.347 --> 00:01:44.608
Whenever you have something like this,

00:01:44.608 --> 00:01:47.638
where you have multiple comparison&nbsp;operators in a row,

00:01:47.638 --> 00:01:51.248
then you basically just put an and between them.

00:01:51.248 --> 00:01:56.590
So, what I had before&nbsp;is equivalent to x is less than y and y is less than z.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.369
Doing it this way can save a little bit of&nbsp;typing, sure.

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:02.404
But one of the main benefits of doing it this way is that

00:02:02.404 --> 00:02:07.150
if I have a temporary variable&nbsp;where I don't really want to give it a name

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:11.840
if I have something like, you know, I call some function&nbsp;and I get the return value of the function,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.529
I don't have to now call this&nbsp;function twice.

00:02:14.529 --> 00:02:19.563
If I wanted to do it just in the old written out way,

00:02:19.563 --> 00:02:22.442
then I would&nbsp;have actually two calls to this function.

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:26.695
And if this function was an expensive function and&nbsp;I didn't want to make two calls

00:02:26.695 --> 00:02:32.142
or if it was something that like had side effects and I didn't&nbsp;want those side effects to happen multiple times,

00:02:32.142 --> 00:02:37.649
then I would sort of be forced to make a temporary&nbsp;variable storing the value of the function call.

00:02:37.649 --> 00:02:43.998
So, allowing these chained comparisons definitely saves&nbsp;on more than just the typing.

00:02:43.998 --> 00:02:47.757
It does prevent you from having to do this kind of pattern here.

00:02:47.757 --> 00:02:53.887
But the language is actually very very flexible about chaining these comparisons together.

00:02:53.887 --> 00:02:58.123
So&nbsp;much so that, I think that most of the time you probably shouldn't use this

00:02:58.123 --> 00:03:03.981
unless you're just&nbsp;doing a very simple comparison like x is less than y is less than z.

00:03:03.981 --> 00:03:07.562
I think intuitively once you&nbsp;understand that this kind of thing is happening,

00:03:07.562 --> 00:03:11.465
it's pretty easy to follow and it can make code&nbsp;more readable even.

00:03:11.465 --> 00:03:16.210
But look at all of the things that you could use in a chain comparison.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:20.341
So, you&nbsp;can use a less than and greater than, less equal, greater equal,

00:03:20.341 --> 00:03:24.844
equals, not equals, is, is not, in and&nbsp;not in.

00:03:24.844 --> 00:03:29.353
All of those things for the purposes of Python are considered comparison operators.

00:03:29.353 --> 00:03:32.348
And they can all be chained together.

00:03:32.348 --> 00:03:35.971
That means that here I could do something like this

00:03:35.971 --> 00:03:39.200
I could say x is less than y is greater than z

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.165
Now mathematically, what is this supposed to mean?

00:03:43.165 --> 00:03:50.578
I mean, I know Python and I know that the rule is that this means x is less than y and y is greater&nbsp;than z.

00:03:50.578 --> 00:03:56.471
But looking at it, just looking at x less than y greater than z

00:03:56.471 --> 00:03:58.836
that no longer really makes&nbsp;sense to me.

00:03:58.836 --> 00:04:01.453
It makes way more sense to have the and in the middle there

00:04:01.453 --> 00:04:05.120
and to have a temporary&nbsp;variable for a function call if I need it.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.145
I don't think it should really even be allowed&nbsp;to mix like less and greater like that in these chained comparisons.

00:04:11.145 --> 00:04:16.788
I don't think you're going to&nbsp;do anything but confuse people if you mix a less and a greater and a chained comparison like this.

00:04:16.788 --> 00:04:21.359
So,&nbsp;let's just go over some examples of what I think would be good use cases

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:25.411
versus questionable versus&nbsp;just outright bad.

00:04:25.411 --> 00:04:28.096
So, here what I would consider good use cases.

00:04:28.096 --> 00:04:32.985
I would argue that in these&nbsp;cases, it's actually more readable to use the chained comparison

00:04:32.985 --> 00:04:38.120
and it agrees with mathematical&nbsp;notation and just looking at it it makes sense what it's doing.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:46.017
It's more readable than what&nbsp;you would have if you wrote out the explicit like 0 is less than x and x is less than 1.

00:04:46.017 --> 00:04:51.287
So, for&nbsp;this one, clearly I'm saying x is strictly between 0 and 1. That makes sense.

00:04:51.287 --> 00:04:54.468
You can express things&nbsp;like closed and open endpoints,

00:04:54.468 --> 00:04:56.786
you can express loose inequalities,

00:04:56.786 --> 00:04:59.016
you can do things the other&nbsp;way around,

00:04:59.016 --> 00:05:00.452
all of these things just make sense.

00:05:00.452 --> 00:05:04.040
It even makes sense to have three things all being&nbsp;equal to each other.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.344
I think what makes these good cases is that

00:05:06.344 --> 00:05:09.983
they're all using sort of the same&nbsp;direction of comparison operator.

00:05:09.983 --> 00:05:15.025
I can still go x is less than or equal to y and y is less than or&nbsp;equal to z.

00:05:15.025 --> 00:05:20.897
So, x is less than equal to z. It makes sense to skip from the very left to the very right.

00:05:20.897 --> 00:05:24.268
And that's because these comparison operators are transitive.

00:05:24.268 --> 00:05:29.347
That's that property. If I have x is&nbsp;less than y and y is less than z, then x is less than z.

00:05:29.347 --> 00:05:32.941
So, as long as you're sort of respecting the&nbsp;transitivity of the operator,

00:05:32.941 --> 00:05:37.833
then I don't really have any problem with chaining the comparisons.

00:05:37.833 --> 00:05:40.907
Okay, let's look at some more questionable examples.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.315
For these examples, I wouldn't necessarily be upset&nbsp;if someone tried to submit a pull request

00:05:48.315 --> 00:05:51.054
that has something like this to one of my codebases.

00:05:51.054 --> 00:05:54.784
But I&nbsp;probably wouldn't write something like this myself.

00:05:54.784 --> 00:05:58.903
Just because I know that when someone sees, you&nbsp;know, a big long expression like this,

00:05:58.903 --> 00:06:00.894
they're going to have to stop and think, like,

00:06:00.894 --> 00:06:03.037
What is even&nbsp;happening here?

00:06:03.037 --> 00:06:05.010
Like, they're going to have to take a second

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:10.999
to realize that this is a case where&nbsp;they need to think about it as a chained comparison.

00:06:10.999 --> 00:06:14.012
What this one is saying is that y and z have to&nbsp;be the same,

00:06:14.012 --> 00:06:17.393
x and y are between 0 and 1 with x less than y.

00:06:17.393 --> 00:06:20.696
It's definitely cluttered and I would&nbsp;consider refactoring to make it more readable. 

00:06:20.696 --> 00:06:23.167
But I wouldn't say that it's bad.

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:26.164
In this example,&nbsp;this part is completely fine.

00:06:26.164 --> 00:06:29.315
And I think that this example is only questionable because

00:06:29.315 --> 00:06:32.398
it's not&nbsp;really clear how the not equals plays into this.

00:06:32.398 --> 00:06:37.600
Is it supposed to be that none of these things&nbsp;are equal to one or just that z isn't equal to 1?

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:41.360
Well, since we're saying that they're all&nbsp;equal, it doesn't really make any difference.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.960
So, that's why I would still include this in an&nbsp;okay to questionable use as opposed to a bad use.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:52.733
So, I think that this example and this example are&nbsp;not actually that questionable.

00:06:52.733 --> 00:06:56.480
I only include them in this questionable use-case scenario

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:03.562
because I&nbsp;think that most people probably aren't aware that is and in are considered comparison operators

00:07:03.562 --> 00:07:07.315
and&nbsp;can participate in this chain comparison operation.

00:07:07.315 --> 00:07:10.762
If you didn't know that is was allowed to&nbsp;participate in comparison,

00:07:10.762 --> 00:07:13.176
I would probably just think this is a syntax error.

00:07:13.176 --> 00:07:15.778
But once I think&nbsp;about it as a comparison,

00:07:15.778 --> 00:07:19.118
then yeah, it makes sense that I'm just saying that x, y and z are all&nbsp;identical.

00:07:19.118 --> 00:07:21.348
They're pointing to the same object.

00:07:21.348 --> 00:07:23.596
There's nothing too radical about any of these&nbsp;examples

00:07:23.596 --> 00:07:27.307
and as long as you have a general awareness that the rule exists,

00:07:27.307 --> 00:07:30.115
then you're not&nbsp;going to be confused by any of these examples.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:37.274
However, let's move on to the: Why are these even&nbsp;allowed examples?

00:07:37.274 --> 00:07:42.246
These are really bad use-cases, in my opinion.

00:07:42.246 --> 00:07:45.674
I'm actually not sure why this is&nbsp;even allowed.

00:07:45.674 --> 00:07:47.825
If you read the documentation,

00:07:47.825 --> 00:07:51.493
it's clear that the person that wrote the documentation&nbsp;knows that this is confusing.

00:07:51.493 --> 00:07:53.991
They say something like: Oh yeah, this is allowed.

00:07:53.991 --> 00:07:58.535
You're allowed to&nbsp;use mixed, like, less than and greater than or any of the comparison operators.

00:07:58.535 --> 00:08:01.684
But it's not pretty&nbsp;is the term that they used.

00:08:01.684 --> 00:08:04.409
So, why this is allowed I'm not quite sure.

00:08:04.409 --> 00:08:08.159
But it's clear that they knew&nbsp;that it was a bad idea, basically.

00:08:08.159 --> 00:08:13.485
My only guess is that it was due to something about the ease&nbsp;of implementing it in the language

00:08:13.485 --> 00:08:16.496
and perhaps due to limitations of the parser.

00:08:16.496 --> 00:08:17.900
Here's another&nbsp;bad example.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:20.480
x is not equal to y is not equal to z

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.620
Now, if you were to write this in a math paper,

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:28.941
it's probably going to be interpreted that none of these things are equal to each other.

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:32.640
not&nbsp;just x is not equal to y and y is not equal to z

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.244
So, in this case, I think this actually disagrees&nbsp;with what your intuition might say.

00:08:37.244 --> 00:08:39.265
My intuition looking at this says:

00:08:39.265 --> 00:08:42.160
This should mean that x, y&nbsp;and z are all distinct.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:45.196
But it actually doesn't have that implication.

00:08:45.196 --> 00:08:49.680
It still could have the&nbsp;possibility that x and z are equal to each other.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.879
And that's what I think makes this an especially&nbsp;bad use.

00:08:52.879 --> 00:08:57.723
I think any time that you use a not equals as part of a chain comparison,

00:08:57.723 --> 00:09:02.060
it's probably going&nbsp;to confuse people, especially if there are multiple not equals.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:05.550
And of course, you're allowed to have as&nbsp;many of these things mixed together as you want.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:09.003
So, you could write something like this and this is&nbsp;perfectly fine.

00:09:09.003 --> 00:09:10.000
Who knows what it means!

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.589
I don't even really want to parse it. I just wrote it to&nbsp;show a bad example.

00:09:13.589 --> 00:09:17.218
And here's the motherload of all bad examples that I could come up with.

00:09:17.218 --> 00:09:20.800
Leave&nbsp;your bad examples in the comments below, by the way.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.535
We're mixing less than and greater than

00:09:23.535 --> 00:09:27.496
and we're&nbsp;mixing the right and left bit shift operators.

00:09:27.496 --> 00:09:32.095
If anyone knows what this code is supposed to do, go&nbsp;ahead tell me.

00:09:32.095 --> 00:09:36.225
Now obviously, no one in their right mind is going to write something this terrible

00:09:36.225 --> 00:09:38.609
except maybe for an obfuscation contest

00:09:38.609 --> 00:09:41.951
where you're supposed to make code look as unreadable&nbsp;as possible.

00:09:41.951 --> 00:09:47.377
But I'm just trying to show this as a point where why is this even allowed in the&nbsp;language.

00:09:47.377 --> 00:09:51.276
Now, I know Python has a lot of sharp corners. It has a lot of features.

00:09:51.276 --> 00:09:55.435
I have a whole&nbsp;video on what I think is the sharpest corner of them all,

00:09:55.435 --> 00:09:59.642
a sort of a hidden thing that Python does&nbsp;that's really unintuitive.

00:09:59.642 --> 00:10:01.279
And that's sort of just expected.

00:10:01.279 --> 00:10:05.490
Every language is going to have these&nbsp;things that are counter-intuitive

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:07.282
or if you don't know the rule,

00:10:07.282 --> 00:10:11.920
then it's not really going to make&nbsp;sense to beginners or you know that kind of thing.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:17.120
But something like this, I just think that&nbsp;really shouldn't be part of the language.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:20.298
Now, I'm not sure how the Python core developers&nbsp;would feel about

00:10:20.298 --> 00:10:22.924
potentially breaking old code that depends on this.

00:10:22.924 --> 00:10:28.078
But I think it is a&nbsp;good idea to probably rein in these chained comparisons

00:10:28.078 --> 00:10:34.493
and not allow things like less&nbsp;than and greater than to be used in the same chained comparison.

00:10:34.493 --> 00:10:38.149
So, I'm not saying that we should&nbsp;get rid of chained comparisons altogether,

00:10:38.149 --> 00:10:42.348
just the ones that inherently don't make sense, like using&nbsp;less than and greater than together.

00:10:42.348 --> 00:10:49.127
On the other hand, I would like to see an expansion of chained comparisons to other cases that do make sense.

00:10:49.127 --> 00:10:54.212
So currently, as of today, if you have something&nbsp;like a NumPy array,

00:10:54.212 --> 00:10:58.817
it's not currently possible to do something like this.

00:10:58.817 --> 00:11:03.952
I can't chain together 3&nbsp;is less than x and x is less than 5.

00:11:03.952 --> 00:11:08.386
So, this kind of thing doesn't actually work in Python today.

00:11:08.386 --> 00:11:12.232
And I think there is a proposal about a way that it could be made to work.

00:11:12.232 --> 00:11:17.924
But that is one direction&nbsp;that I think that it would be okay if we expanded chained comparisons.

00:11:17.924 --> 00:11:19.759
And that's all I've got. Thanks&nbsp;for watching.

00:11:19.759 --> 00:11:22.723
If you liked the video don't forget to like, comment and subscribe.

00:11:22.723 --> 00:11:24.942
Special shout out&nbsp;to all my patrons and donors.

